        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe placement is good and mostly idiomatic. Before the one finding, things I checked and found clean:\n\n- `riskFlag` is in the right unit. It aggregates three owner-level read-time signals and sits alongside its peers (`bulkSignupWarning`, `capacityWarning`), reusing `capacityWarning(owner)` rather than re-deriving it. Natural home.\n- `isDisposableAdjacent` belongs in `OwnerEmail` \u2014 it's an email-domain notion, next to `isDisposable`/`isValid`/`normalize`, consistent with that class's stated \"single source of truth for email notions\" role.\n- The fully-qualified `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.OwnerEmail...` reference and the widening of `OwnerEmail` to `public` are *not* smells here \u2014 they match the established convention in this exact file, which already reaches into that package with FQNs (`OwnerMemberId`, `FiscalYear`, `OwnerLocality`, `OwnerIdentity`, `OwnerTelephone` at lines 41\u201343, 204, 219, 230, 247, 260).\n\nFinding (duplication in `OwnerEmail`):\n\n- `isDisposableAdjacent` (lines 66\u201380) re-implements the \"extract the domain from a normalized email\" sequence that `isDisposable` (lines 50\u201357) already does: `normalize(email)` \u2192 `lastIndexOf('@')` \u2192 `at < 0` guard \u2192 `substring(at + 1)`. That's the same four-line notion inlined twice, in the very class whose javadoc says these notions should \"stay defined in one place.\"\n- Suggested fix: extract a private `String domainOf(String email)` (returning the domain, or `null` when no `@`) and have both `isDisposable` and `isDisposableAdjacent` call it. `isDisposable` becomes `domain != null && DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS.contains(domain)`; `isDisposableAdjacent` keeps its present/valid guard, then `domain != null && !DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS.contains(domain) && DISPOSABLE_BASE_LABELS.contains(baseLabel(domain))`. This keeps \"the domain of an email\" as one defined notion alongside `baseLabel`, matching the class's design intent."
